# Enforce LF line endings workspace-wide, independent of the developer's
# `git config core.autocrlf` setting. Critical for:
# - Rust source files: `include_str!` + source-scanning tests (e.g.
#   pcloud-proto/src/tls.rs `shared_config_rejects_tls_1_2`) assume LF
#   line endings. Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` will
#   inject CRLF and break those tests.
# - KAT fixtures: crypto ciphertext/plaintext pairs must be byte-for-byte
#   identical across platforms.
# - SSH keys, test certificates, PEM fixtures: CRLF corrupts them.
#
# Binary files (images, archives, compiled artifacts) should be treated
# as binary so Git never tries to normalize their content.

*                text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit binary file types — belt-and-braces against `text=auto`
# mis-sniffing.
*.png            binary
*.jpg            binary
*.jpeg           binary
*.gif            binary
*.ico            binary
*.bin            binary
*.blob           binary
*.key            binary
*.pdf            binary
*.zip            binary
*.gz             binary
*.tar            binary
*.tgz            binary
*.bz2            binary
*.xz             binary
*.7z             binary
*.so             binary
*.dylib          binary
*.dll            binary
*.exe            binary

# Rust source + test fixtures: force LF (override any OS default).
*.rs             text eol=lf
*.toml           text eol=lf
*.md             text eol=lf
*.yml            text eol=lf
*.yaml           text eol=lf
*.json           text eol=lf
*.csv            text eol=lf
*.sh             text eol=lf
*.ps1            text eol=lf
*.py             text eol=lf
